<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1976 CE – present · capital Tifariti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A partially recognised state in the western Maghreb that claims all of Western Sahara but controls only its eastern part; Morocco holds the rest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a>, formed in 1973, fought Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-sahara">Spanish Sahara</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> Accords on 14 November 1975, and Moroccan and Mauritanian forces occupied the territory after Spain withdrew by 12 January 1976. The Polisario Front proclaimed the republic on 27 February 1976 at Bir Lehlou. Its recognition is partial: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> does not recognise it, and Morocco calls the land it holds its Southern Provinces.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic is officially a one-party semi-presidential republic, and the secretary-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polisario-front">Polisario Front</a> has been its president since August 1982. Its government works largely from the refugee camps near Tindouf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> later withdrew its claim. A constitution of 1999 provides for a multi-party state after independence. The republic has been a full member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> since 1982. By September 2022 it had been recognised by 85 states, 39 of which had frozen or withdrawn recognition.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The republic claims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a> but controls only the area east of the Moroccan wall, which it calls the Liberated Territories. Its claimed capital is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laayoune">Laayoune</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> holds, and the provisional capital moved to Tifariti in 2008. Brahim Ghali is its president. Morocco proposed autonomy for the territory in 2007, while the republic seeks a referendum on independence.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic.jpg" alt="Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: El Uali Mustafá Sayed from Polisario Front · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sahrawi_Arab_Democratic_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1976 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tifariti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brahim Ghali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (Maliki school of Sunni Islam)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Modern Standard Arabic, with Hassaniya Arabic and Spanish widely used</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sahrawi Republic, SADR</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2001&amp;focus=sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic" aria-label="Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4401 1217 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4721 1473L4655 1514"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4655 1514h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4721 1473h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4721 1473h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tifariti. <a href="/atlas?year=2001&amp;focus=sahrawi-arab-democratic-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
